          %h2#relation_to_speculative_philosophy Relation to speculative philosophy
          %p
            According to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"} Georg Hegel
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+the+Philosophical+Sciences"} Encyclopedia of the Philosophical Sciences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/speculative+philosophy"} speculative philosophy
            of the 19th century:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#Enc82d"} Enc§82d
              Hinsichtlich der Bedeutung des Spekulativen ist hier noch zu erwähnen, daß man darunter dasselbe zu verstehen hat, was früher, zumal in Beziehung auf das religiöse Bewußtsein und dessen Inhalt, als das Mystische bezeichnet zu werden pflegte. Wenn heutzutage vom Mystischen die Rede ist, so gilt dies in der Regel als gleichbedeutend mit dem Geheimnisvollen und Unbegreiflichen, und dies Geheimnisvolle und Unbegreifliche wird dann, je nach Verschiedenheit der sonstigen Bildung und Sinnesweise, von den einen als das Eigentliche und Wahrhafte, von den anderen aber als das dem Aberglauben und der Täuschung Angehörige betrachtet.
            %p Hierüber ist zunächst zu bemerken, daß das Mystische allerdings ein Geheimnisvolles ist, jedoch nur für den Verstand, und zwar einfach um deswillen, weil die abstrakte Identität das Prinzip des Verstandes, das Mystische aber (als gleichbedeutend mit dem Spekulativen) die konkrete Einheit derjenigen Bestimmungen ist, welche dem Verstand nur in ihrer Trennung und Entgegensetzung für wahr gelten. Wenn dann diejenigen, welche das Mystische als das Wahrhafte anerkennen, es gleichfalls dabei bewenden lassen, daß dasselbe ein schlechthin Geheimnisvolles sei, so wird damit ihrerseits nur ausgesprochen, daß das Denken für sie gleichfalls nur die Bedeutung des abstrakten Identischsetzens hat und daß man um deswillen, um zur Wahrheit zu gelangen, auf das Denken verzichten oder, wie auch gesagt zu werden pflegt, daß man die Vernunft gefangennehmen müsse.
            %p
              %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#Enc82e"} Enc§82e
              Nun aber ist, wie wir gesehen haben, das abstrakt verständige Denken so wenig ein Festes und Letztes, daß dasselbe sich vielmehr als das beständige Aufheben seiner selbst und als das Umschlagen in sein Entgegengesetztes erweist, wohingegen das Vernünftige als solches gerade darin besteht, die Entgegengesetzten als ideelle Momente in sich zu enthalten. Alles Vernünftige ist somit zugleich als mystisch zu bezeichnen, womit jedoch nur so viel gesagt ist, daß dasselbe über den Verstand hinausgeht, und keineswegs, daß dasselbe überhaupt als dem Denken unzugänglich und unbegreiflich zu betrachten sei.
            %p It should also be mentioned here that the meaning of the speculative is to be understood as being the same as what used in earlier times to be called “mystical”, especially with regard to the religious consciousness and its content. When we speak of the “mystical” nowadays, it is taken as a rule to be synonymous with what is mysterious and incomprehensible; and, depending on the ways their culture and mentality vary in other respects, some people treat the mysterious and in­comprehensible as what is authentic and genuine, whilst others regard it as belong­ing to the domain of superstition and deception. About this we must remark first that “the mystical” is certainly something mysterious, but only for the understand­ing, and then only because abstract identity is the principle of the understanding. But when it is regarded as synonymous with the speculative, the mystical is the concrete unity of just those determinations that count as true for the understanding only in their separation and opposition. So if those who recognise the mystical as what is genuine say that it is something utterly mysterious, and just leave it at that, they are only declaring that for them, too, thinking has only the Significance of an abstract positing of identity, and that in order to attain the truth we must renounce thinking, or, as they frequently put it, that we must “take reason captive.” As we have seen, however, the abstract thinking of the understanding is so far from being something firm and ultimate that it proves itself, on the contrary, to be a constant sublating of itself and an overturning into its opposite, whereas the rational as such is rational precisely because it contains both of the opposites as ideal moments within itself. Thus, everything rational can equally be called “mystical”; but this only amounts to saying that it transcends the understanding. It does not at all imply that what is so spoken of must be considered inaccessible to thinking and incomprehensible.
          %p
            For Hegel’s relation to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meister+Eckhart"} Meister Eckhart
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "Meister+Eckhart#RelationToHegel"} there
          %h2#bertrand_russells_relation_to_mysticism Bertrand Russell’s relation to mysticism
          %p
            The philosopher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"} Bertrand Russell
            concerned himself with mysticism and was, according to himself, influenced by a “mathematical mysticism” in his younger years.
          %p
            In his essay “Mysticism and Logic” (
            %a{:href => "#Russell18"}> Russell 1918
            ) Russell understands mysticism as “the attempt to conceive the world as a whole by means of thought”. He gives four characteristics of it:
          %ol
            %li “belief in insight as against discursive knowledge”
            %li a belief in unity of everything
            %li denial of the reality of time
            %li the claim that evil, and sometimes also good, is a mere appearance
          %p
            Russell finds at least some of these characteristics in the philosophies of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heraclitus"} Heraclitus
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Plato"} Plato
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spinoza"} Spinoza
          %p
            In the autobiographical book
            %em Portraits from Memory
            the passage
          %blockquote
            %p Mathematics … deals with the world of ideas and has in consequence an exactness and perfection which is absent from the everyday world. This kind of mathematical mysticism, which Plato derived from Pythagoras, appealed to me.
          %p
            may serve as an example how Russell was influenced in young age by the exposure to mathematics; more poetically it is expressed in the essay
            %em The Study of Mathematics
            published along with
            %em Mysticism and Logic
            (
            %a{:href => "#Russell18"}> Russell 1918
            )
          %blockquote
            %p Mathematics, rightly viewed, possesses not only truth, but supreme beauty—a beauty cold and austere, like that of sculpture, without the gorgeous trappings of painting or music, yet sublimely pure, and capable of a stern perfection such as only the greatest art can show. The true spirit of delight, the exaltation, the sense of being more than man, which is the touchstone of the highest excellence, is to be found in mathematics as surely as in poetry. … Real life is, to most men, a long second-best, a perpetual compromise between the ideal and the possible; but the world of pure reason knows no compromise, no practical limitations, no barrier to the creative activity embodying in splendid edifices the passionate aspiration after the perfect from which all great works springs.
            %p … Of the austere virtues the love of truth is the chief, and in mathematics, more than elsewhere, the love of truth may find encouragement for waning faith.
          %p
            According to Ray
            %a{:href => "#Monk97"} Monk’s biography
            on Russell, the search for a foundation of this “Pythagorean” mysticism governed the first half of his life: Being deeply disappointed by the mathematical curriculum at Cambridge, which did not address his philosophical needs, Russell turned to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idealism"} idealism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.M.+Ellis+McTaggart"} J.M. Ellis McTaggart
            following the German tradition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kant"} Kant
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/An+Essay+on+the+Foundations+of+Geometry"} An Essay on the Foundations of Geometry
            a variation of Kant’s transcendental aesthetics claiming that the curvature of the space of our perception, i.e. of any ‘’form of externality’’, is a priori constant.
          %p
            Exposure to the work of the German mathematicians
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Dedekind
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karl+Weierstra%C3%9F"} Weierstraß
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Cantor
            along with a personal meeting with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano"} Peano
            caused a turn in Russell’s views. He now hoped to find a rigorous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundation of mathematics
            based solely on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/platonism"} Platonic
            view on mathematics. But over the years he was frustrated in this attempt by many problems he encountered, especially
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell%27s+paradox"} Russell's paradox
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wittgenstein"} Wittgenstein's
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tractatus+Logico-Philosophicus"} Tractatus
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formalism"} formalism
          %p He remarks on the power of rigorous mathematical definition:
          %blockquote
            %p
              “Continuity” had been, until he [Cantor] defined it, a vague word, convenient for philosophers like Hegel, who wished to introduce metaphysical muddles into mathematics. Cantor gave a precise significance to the word, and showed that continuity, as he defined it, was the concept needed by mathematicians and physicist. By this means a great deal of mysticism, such as that of Bergson, was rendered antiquated. (Chapter XXXI of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+History+of+Western+Philosophy"}> A History of Western Philosophy
              ” (1945))
          %h2#brouwer Brouwer
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.E.J.+Brouwer"} L.E.J. Brouwer
            had a long standing interest in the theory and practice of mysticism, setting out his manifesto while still a graduate student in the essay
            %a{:href => "#LAM05"} Life, Art, and Mysticism
            (1905). The essay quotes the Bhagavad Gita,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meister+Eckhart"} Meister Eckhart
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+B%C3%B6hme"} Jakob Böhme
          %p
            However, he did not consider his
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"} intuitionism
            to depend upon his mystical views as such. For Brouwer, mysticism is the domain of “consciousness’ deepest home”, wherein there is no subject-object distinction. The first act of intuitionism, the intuition of time, propels us away from this pure state of “wisdom”. It is in fact the precondition for any experience of an outer world or experience of the self as something distinct. Mathematics thus comes into being in this exodus from the home of consciousness, so on Brouwer’s view, “mathematics is in a sense the very opposite of mysticism” (
            %a{:href => "#vanAtten04"}> Van Atten, 2004
            ), and mystical insight is only possible quite apart from the machinations of the intellect.
          %p Brouwer even went so far as to remark on the perniciousness of mathematics in its keeping us from the home of consciousness. Thus in the notebooks in which he conceived his 1907 thesis we find: “One could see as the goal of one’s life: abolition and delivery from all mathematics”.
          %h2#gdel Gödel
          %p
            For the moment, see e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#vAT14"} van Atten and Tragesser 2014
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6del%27s_ontological_proof"} Gödel’s ontological proof
            involving
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hermeticism"}> hermeticism
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gnosticism"} gnosticism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meister+Eckhart"} Meister Eckhart
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mysticism"} Mysticism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+the+Philosophy+of+Religion"} Lectures on the Philosophy of Religion
                1832
            %li#LAM05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.E.J.+Brouwer"}> L.E.J. Brouwer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.ndjfl/1039886518"}
                    %em Life, Art, and Mysticism
            %li#Russell18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"}> Bertrand Russell
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mysticism and Logic and Other Essays
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mysticism_and_Logic_and_Other_Essays"} on Wikisource
                %em Mysticism and Logic
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %em The Study of Mathematics
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Hibbert Journal
            %li#CPM
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.+E.+J.+Brouwer"}> L. E. J. Brouwer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jsl/1183730638"}
                    %em Consciousness, Philosophy, and Mathematics
            %li#Monk97
              %p
                Ray Monk,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bertrand Russell – Mathematics: Dreams and Nightmares
            %li
              %p
                van Atten &amp; Tragesser,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.99.1853&rep=rep1&type=pdf"} Mysticism and mathematics: Brouwer, Gödel, and the common core thesis
            %li#vanAtten04
              %p
                van Atten,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On Brouwer
            %li#Melzer14
              %p
                Arthur Melzer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Philosophy between the lines – The lost history of esoteric writing
            %li#vAT14
              %p
                Mark van Atten, Robert Tragesser:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mysticism and Mathematics: Brouwer, Gödel, and the Common Core Thesis
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Essays on Gödel’s Reception of Leibniz, Husserl, and Brouwer
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 35
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-10031-9_10"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-10031-9_10
          %p
            Decent discussion of the history of mysticism (or not) in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction+collapse"} wavefunction collapse
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
          %ul
